    "page_id": "topic-6-10-5",
    "page_path": "/explore/business/software-delivery-and-operations/log-collection-and-analysis/",
    "name": "Log Collection & Analysis",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/business/software-delivery-and-operations/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Log collection brings event records from applications and infrastructure into systems where they can be searched and interpreted. Reliable analysis depends on consistent fields, storage that survives workload failures, and controls that keep sensitive information out of records.",
    "tags": ["Logging", "Observability", "OpenTelemetry", "Kubernetes", "Data protection"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T19:20:07Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T19:20:07Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Log Collection and Analysis | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Learn how event logs are structured, collected, retained, and protected, with clear limits on evidence about Iranian adoption."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "records-and-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Making events understandable",
        "content": "A log records an event at a particular time. OpenTelemetry, a project that standardizes the collection and exchange of telemetry, describes logs as timestamped records with optional metadata. Their value increases when records from different components can be interpreted consistently. A message saying that an operation failed becomes more useful when its source and relevant execution context are also available. [s1]\n\nStructured logging means maintaining consistent field names, types, and meanings. Merely encoding a message as JSON does not guarantee that consistency. OpenTelemetry supports integration with existing logging frameworks and correlation with other telemetry signals. Its Collector can receive records, process their contents, and export them onward. This allows a collection pipeline to normalize different inputs before analysis, rather than requiring every application to produce an identical original format. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "collection-and-survival",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Keeping records beyond the life of a workload",
        "content": "Container platforms make the distinction between producing and retaining logs especially important. Kubernetes captures container output, but its documentation explains that a complete logging solution needs more than the runtime's native facilities. Operators may need records after a container crashes, a Pod is evicted, or a node fails. Cluster-level logging therefore gives records a storage lifecycle independent of those components. [s2]\n\nKubernetes does not supply a native cluster-level log storage backend. Documented approaches include an agent on each node, a companion container alongside an application, or direct delivery from application code. A node agent can collect container output and forward it for aggregation without changing each application. These approaches distribute collection responsibilities differently; selecting one does not by itself establish that records will remain available after a failure. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "sensitive-data",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Useful evidence without exposing secrets",
        "content": "Logging can create a second location for information that was carefully protected elsewhere. OWASP's logging guidance identifies passwords, access tokens, encryption keys, database connection strings, sensitive personal information, and payment-related data as material that should generally not be recorded directly. Depending on the information and purpose, exclusion, masking, sanitization, or another protective treatment may be appropriate. [s3]\n\nThe aim is selective evidence rather than indiscriminate copying. A diagnostic record can identify the kind of failure without reproducing the credential or complete request body involved. OWASP also cautions that collecting certain information or monitoring communications can raise legal issues. Its guidance is a general security reference, not a statement of Iran's retention requirements or authorization rules. Those require separate, current legal research. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What remains to be established for Iran",
        "content": "These sources provide a technical basis for examining logging services used by Iranian organizations. They do not identify which platforms dominate locally, how organizations operate them, or whether particular services satisfy their users' needs. A fuller national account requires documented deployments, clearly defined provider capabilities, and evidence about retention and access practices. Until that work is completed, product documentation should explain mechanisms rather than stand in for an assessment of Iran's market.",
        "source_ids": []
      },
      {
        "id": "log-pipeline",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "From event production to investigation",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Application event\"] -->|\"Produces record\"| B[\"Logging framework\"]\n  B -->|\"Emits records\"| C[\"Collector receiver\"]\n  C -->|\"Passes records\"| D[\"Processing and normalization\"]\n  D -->|\"Exports records\"| E[\"Storage and analysis backend\"]\n  E -->|\"Returns relevant records\"| F[\"Incident investigation\"]\n```",
        "summary": "This general pipeline separates record production, collection, processing, and analysis. It does not represent a verified Iranian deployment.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
